2026-09-21: AArch64 ELF function placement acceptance.
Source: function-section-boundaries.c
Source SHA256 cf6a512a74ee63100c0fd9e7107703055e9424518b9c50a2efe6ffe946f8d8eb
EvoCC release SHA256 4ec65ed2395fafc7c4b8f0728d3489ae29340a5cd1e3c98d649a5020ae2d866f
EvoIR 1.9.104 / EvoMIR 0.34; HEAD alone does not identify this dirty tree.

Offline sealed linux/arm64, GCC 14.2.0-19 and Clang 19.1.7:
  cc -std=gnu11 -O{0,2} -c function-section-boundaries.c -o probe.o
  readelf -SW probe.o; readelf -sW probe.o
  cc probe.o -o probe; ./probe
Both compilers at O0 and O2: late in .exit.text, boot and boot_alias in
.init.text, ordinary in .text, no standalone inlined symbol; exit 0.
The patchable entry table has WAL flags and links to the code section.

EvoCC --target=aarch64-unknown-linux-gnu -std=gnu11 --emit=asm produces
assembly accepted by BOTH gcc-14 and clang-19; both linked programs exit 0.
late: .exit.text, offset 0, size 40.
boot and boot_alias: .init.text, offset 64, size 100, section alignment 64.
ordinary: .text, offset 0, size 40. No .discard.inline section is emitted.
The static label relocation and indirect jump execute successfully.
__patchable_function_entries remains WAL and link-ordered to .init.text.
The callable-symbol alignment, unlike the oracle's offset 8 for this
patchable-entry combination, follows the already recorded project profile;
this is not claimed as byte-for-byte oracle code layout equivalence.
Both C-HIR and EvoIR interpreters return 0.

Boundary (same source, -DLATE_SECTION_ONLY):
GCC-14 O0/O2: late in .exit.text.
Clang-19 O0/O2: late in .text; warning: attribute declaration must precede
definition [-Wignored-attributes]. Neither compiler rejects compilation.
EvoCC: E2943 invalid-or-conflicting-symbol-attribute, exit 1, no assembly.
The two-oracle disagreement is deliberately fail-closed, not counted as
an agreed placement. Identical late repetitions of an earlier request pass.

Two fixture programs (this and kernel-function-section.c), two assemblers:
4 native executions PASS; both interpreters for both fixtures: 4 PASS.
These are targeted placement/runtime results, not a full-kernel link verdict.

Final encoding correction (same date):
Release SHA256 73eff5c4835db643339c865da49698440202d4e40c75816e2951436c02e4a46b
Added function-section-utf8.c, SHA256
d9d967ac6719d0a73b4bf3da1f6224efd3aa28aff4bd13ebfec5cd4958316040.
RED: ordinary UTF-8 had incorrectly become .text.<C3><83><C2><A9>.
The decoded source units now form UTF-8 bytes once. The MIR regression also
requires raw UTF-8 in assembler text: GNU as decodes octal section escapes,
but LLVM retains those escapes literally, despite successful execution.
GREEN: both assemblers' ELF section is exactly .text.<C3><A9>, AX, align 4.
Both oracles at O0/O2 agree on raw/adjacent UTF-8 placement and return 0.
The numeric-escape variant (-DNUMERIC_SECTION_ESCAPE) is accepted by GCC
but rejected by Clang as an invalid escape in an unevaluated string literal;
EvoCC explicitly rejects that function-attribute spelling with E2943.
These source spelling restrictions do not restrict canonical IR hex escapes.

Final release reruns all THREE fixtures: 6/6 interpreter executions and
6/6 GCC/Clang-assembled native executions return 0; ELF placement is inspected
separately. Both late-only and numeric-escape negative variants are E2943.
The preliminary full attempt 20260921T031718Z and initial workspace gate were
interrupted to include the discovered encoding fixes. No verified full batch
or new link/boot verdict existed; interrupted attempts are not counted PASS.

